-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/78815d06-d662-4287-8013-8aaf0a6b9a32
-- title:
--   Prime metadata survives preserved memory
-- statement:
--   Let $\mu,\nu:\mathbb N\to\mathbb Z$ agree below an address bound $b$. Let $f,M,y\in\mathbb N$, let $q:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ be the stored modulus-prefix sequence, and put $d=\operatorname{depth}(M,y)$. Suppose $\mu$ satisfies the prime-metadata storage specification: a six-cell header at $f$, the prime-pool values at $f+6$, and $q(0),\ldots,q(d)$ at $f+6+3y$. If
--
--   $$
--   f+6+3y+d+1\le b,
--   $$
--
--   then $\nu$ satisfies the same storage specification for $f,M,y,q$.
--
--   This preserves the retained common-prime metadata through computations whose writes leave its allocated prefix unchanged.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/EncodedColorPrimeData.lean#L37-L51

